 A real sensor bridge can call `setDrive()` at ~20 Hz; nothing else in the
 code needs to change.
 
+## Sensor bridge (CENTAD EMG node)
+
+Opt-in. Add `?ws=<url>` and the display connects to a WebSocket and calls
+`setDrive(act)` for every message that carries a numeric `act` (0–1):
+
+| URL | Effect |
+| --- | --- |
+| `/` | No bridge. Behaviour unchanged. |
+| `/?ws=ws://centad-emg.local:81` | Connect to the EMG node (Pico 2 W). |
+| `/?ws=auto` | Connect to `ws://<this page's host>:81`; use when the Pico serves the page. |
+| `/?ws=auto&debug=1` | As above, plus the debug overlay, which now shows WS state, message rate, last `act` and the sensor status. |
+
+If no data arrives for more than 1 s (cable off, Wi-Fi dropped, Pico
+rebooted) drive ramps to 0 over 0.5 s, so the dog always comes to rest.
+The socket reconnects automatically (0.5 s → 5 s back-off) and straight away
+when the iPad wakes the tab.
+
+**Mixed content:** browsers refuse a `ws://` socket from an `https://` page,
+so the bridge cannot work on the Vercel deployment; the overlay will read
+`ws blocked`. On the rig, open the copy the Pico serves over plain HTTP
+instead: `http://centad-emg.local/` (the Pico redirects that to `/?ws=auto`).
+The Vercel site keeps working as a sensor-less demo.
+
 ## Deploy to Vercel
 
 ```
